1
00:00:02,560 --> 00:00:05,840
Now that we understand how context works,
it's time to take advantage of it.

2
00:00:05,840 --> 00:00:08,002
If you want to get your agents to behave
certain

3
00:00:08,002 --> 00:00:10,614
ways and do certain things and stop making
silly mistakes,

4
00:00:10,614 --> 00:00:11,920
this is where we'll learn it.

5
00:00:11,920 --> 00:00:15,782
The concept that we're going to talk about
mostly is the idea of memory systems,

6
00:00:15,782 --> 00:00:17,520
but more importantly, my spin on it.

7
00:00:17,520 --> 00:00:20,080
Your knowledge system should just be your
code base.

8
00:00:20,080 --> 00:00:22,349
Throughout the years of building with
agents,

9
00:00:22,349 --> 00:00:26,131
there's been a lot of back and forth in how
we should encode our knowledge,

10
00:00:26,131 --> 00:00:28,400
everything from docs to beads to rules to
dot

11
00:00:28,400 --> 00:00:32,080
cursor files that we would have in our
repos, all these different things.

12
00:00:32,080 --> 00:00:34,770
Instead of focusing on these specific
implementation details,

13
00:00:34,770 --> 00:00:37,063
I want to focus more on how to work with a
knowledge

14
00:00:37,063 --> 00:00:39,840
system and how to make your agents behave
better by using them.

15
00:00:39,840 --> 00:00:42,000
I want to start here with a simple exercise.

16
00:00:42,000 --> 00:00:44,402
Think back to the last time you were
onboarded

17
00:00:44,402 --> 00:00:46,960
onto a big code base you hadn't worked on
before.

18
00:00:46,960 --> 00:00:48,550
Whether this was at your job or you

19
00:00:48,550 --> 00:00:51,004
were trying to contribute to some open
source project,

20
00:00:51,004 --> 00:00:52,640
I'm imagining pre-agents, obviously.

21
00:00:52,640 --> 00:00:56,315
But remember that feeling that you got when
you first opened the repo,

22
00:00:56,315 --> 00:00:59,360
not understanding where anything was or what
was going on?

23
00:00:59,360 --> 00:01:01,680
I used to give a lot of advice on how to get
through this.

24
00:01:01,680 --> 00:01:03,319
It was a combination of techniques,

25
00:01:03,319 --> 00:01:05,800
mostly starting from the idea that learning
the whole

26
00:01:05,800 --> 00:01:08,000
code base was never the right way to do
things.

27
00:01:08,000 --> 00:01:11,746
Nobody ever contributed to a codebase the
size of Amazon or Googles by

28
00:01:11,746 --> 00:01:16,080
reading every single file in it line by line
until they built that understanding.

29
00:01:16,080 --> 00:01:17,260
We got it other ways,

30
00:01:17,260 --> 00:01:21,360
usually from seeing what was changing and
how the pieces worked together.

31
00:01:21,360 --> 00:01:23,840
And that's what we want our agents to have
too.

32
00:01:23,840 --> 00:01:26,065
Remember, every time you start a new thread,

33
00:01:26,065 --> 00:01:30,160
that agent is effectively having its memory
wiped and it's starting from scratch.

34
00:01:30,160 --> 00:01:32,543
And our goal isn't to make sure everything

35
00:01:32,543 --> 00:01:35,040
it could ever possibly need is in its brain.

36
00:01:35,040 --> 00:01:37,676
Our goal is to make sure that the right
paths exist to

37
00:01:37,676 --> 00:01:40,800
help our agent find what it needs when it's
making real changes.

38
00:01:40,800 --> 00:01:42,878
Back in the day when I was trying to onboard

39
00:01:42,878 --> 00:01:45,334
our new hires to the codebases at Amazon and
Twitch,

40
00:01:45,334 --> 00:01:47,176
I had a silly trick that I used to help

41
00:01:47,176 --> 00:01:49,680
get through a lot of the friction for the
onboarding.

42
00:01:49,680 --> 00:01:52,160
I called it the Dumb Questions rule.

43
00:01:52,160 --> 00:01:54,000
The point of this rule was simple.

44
00:01:54,000 --> 00:01:56,960
Everybody's kinda scared of seeming dumb,
especially when they ask questions.

45
00:01:56,960 --> 00:01:58,480
Nobody likes feeling dumb.

46
00:01:58,480 --> 00:02:01,118
We've historically addressed this problem by
trying to go the

47
00:02:01,118 --> 00:02:03,280
angle of there's no such thing as a dumb
question.

48
00:02:03,280 --> 00:02:04,400
I think that's silly.

49
00:02:04,400 --> 00:02:06,640
Everybody has asked a question that's dumb.

50
00:02:06,640 --> 00:02:08,800
I would rather just have them ask it.

51
00:02:08,800 --> 00:02:11,630
So when I onboard a new employees at my
other roles,

52
00:02:11,630 --> 00:02:15,440
I implemented the dumb question rule, which
was meant to inverse this.

53
00:02:15,440 --> 00:02:17,280
The dumb question rule was simple.

54
00:02:17,280 --> 00:02:21,754
Every day you were on payroll, you had to
ask me or one of the other leads

55
00:02:21,754 --> 00:02:25,200
at least one question that made you feel
dumb for asking.

56
00:02:25,200 --> 00:02:28,320
Until you had asked a question that made you
feel kind of stupid,

57
00:02:28,320 --> 00:02:29,520
your workday wasn't done.

58
00:02:29,520 --> 00:02:32,134
Some people would do five in their first few
hours,

59
00:02:32,134 --> 00:02:36,388
some would wait until the end of the day to
write like a really good dumb question,

60
00:02:36,388 --> 00:02:38,797
but I found that actively encouraging
employees

61
00:02:38,797 --> 00:02:40,950
to ask these types of questions especially

62
00:02:40,950 --> 00:02:44,640
new hires was incredibly useful so how does
any of this apply to agents?

63
00:02:44,640 --> 00:02:48,395
Well you're effectively hiring a world-class
engineer that's never seen your

64
00:02:48,395 --> 00:02:51,360
code base before every time you click the
new thread button.

65
00:02:51,360 --> 00:02:53,685
So one of the best things you can do is

66
00:02:53,685 --> 00:02:57,440
have them ask dumb questions or ask it dumb
questions yourself.

67
00:02:57,440 --> 00:02:59,520
I'm gonna demo this in a pretty silly way.

68
00:02:59,520 --> 00:03:04,263
I'm gonna take a real-world large code base,
in this case the T3 code code base,

69
00:03:04,263 --> 00:03:08,354
and I'm going to delete the README, the
CLAUDE.md, and the AGENTS.md,

70
00:03:08,354 --> 00:03:12,800
which is going to effectively lob off the
context from the top of the repo.

71
00:03:12,800 --> 00:03:15,333
So now when I spin up an agent in it and ask
it things,

72
00:03:15,333 --> 00:03:17,360
it's not going to have what it used to have.

73
00:03:17,360 --> 00:03:22,560
So I'm going to ask it something simple like
I want to work with you in this repo.

74
00:03:22,560 --> 00:03:29,520
I want you to ask me questions about what it
is and how it works.

75
00:03:29,520 --> 00:03:36,080
What confusion arises as you start to
explore the code base?

76
00:03:36,080 --> 00:03:38,726
Sadly, this type of dumb question doesn't
make a whole lot

77
00:03:38,726 --> 00:03:41,600
of sense to ask anymore as I've just
accidentally demonstrated.

78
00:03:41,600 --> 00:03:43,226
So I'm going to show you some examples

79
00:03:43,226 --> 00:03:45,280
that have actually been really beneficial to
me.

80
00:03:45,280 --> 00:03:48,160
Here's a real example that I did just a few
days ago.

81
00:03:48,160 --> 00:03:50,624
I was working on a video about Gemini 4
Argon and

82
00:03:50,624 --> 00:03:53,440
I was getting a lot of data for my own real-
world usage.

83
00:03:53,520 --> 00:03:56,160
And I was asking questions about it, like
how does it behave?

84
00:03:56,160 --> 00:03:58,720
How is it acting compared to all these other
models?

85
00:03:58,720 --> 00:04:01,840
And I got this nice little visualizer that
it wrote for T3 code.

86
00:04:01,840 --> 00:04:02,800
There's a problem though.

87
00:04:02,800 --> 00:04:06,328
I don't know how well this will come up when
the video is encoded,

88
00:04:06,328 --> 00:04:10,230
but uh it's very clear to me right now there
is a strong black background

89
00:04:10,230 --> 00:04:14,560
around this embedded HTML that is different
from the gray that we use in T3 code.

90
00:04:14,560 --> 00:04:18,753
This is because the model wrote HTML for
this response and gave it a hard,

91
00:04:18,753 --> 00:04:20,000
true black background.

92
00:04:20,000 --> 00:04:20,800
It is hideous.

93
00:04:20,800 --> 00:04:23,360
And I had seen it doing this earlier the
same day.

94
00:04:23,360 --> 00:04:26,400
And here is where it's important to start
asking questions.

95
00:04:26,400 --> 00:04:30,560
Why do you always add awful black
backgrounds to your inline HTML?

96
00:04:30,560 --> 00:04:34,000
Whatever makes you do that, fix it here and
push it to the fleet.

97
00:04:34,000 --> 00:04:36,757
This one was convenient because this repo,
my fleet repo,

98
00:04:36,757 --> 00:04:40,240
is where I manage and maintain all of my
skills for all of my harnesses.

99
00:04:40,240 --> 00:04:41,920
More about that in the next two lessons.

100
00:04:41,920 --> 00:04:43,923
But I could have just asked this first part,

101
00:04:43,923 --> 00:04:47,200
why do you always add these awful black
backgrounds to your inline HTML?

102
00:04:47,200 --> 00:04:48,880
At the end here it said why?

103
00:04:48,880 --> 00:04:51,360
The global true black rule was too broad.

104
00:04:51,360 --> 00:04:53,681
I scoped it to product UI and added an

105
00:04:53,681 --> 00:04:57,040
explicit rule that inline HTML inherits the
chat theme.

106
00:04:57,040 --> 00:05:02,240
This is a change it had made to my core file
skill for this particular thing.

107
00:05:02,240 --> 00:05:05,360
And I'm gonna be real with you, it wasn't a
very good fix.

108
00:05:05,360 --> 00:05:09,887
The agents are more than capable of telling
you why they did something wrong,

109
00:05:09,887 --> 00:05:13,120
but they don't always make the right changes
to fix it.

110
00:05:13,120 --> 00:05:14,927
So when I ask these questions,

111
00:05:14,927 --> 00:05:18,480
it isn't necessarily because I want it to go
fix it itself.

112
00:05:18,480 --> 00:05:19,695
We might get to the point in the

113
00:05:19,695 --> 00:05:21,708
future where the models can write really
good skills,

114
00:05:21,708 --> 00:05:24,139
write really good markdown to instruct
themselves in the future,

115
00:05:24,139 --> 00:05:26,000
write really good prompts, those types of
things.

116
00:05:26,000 --> 00:05:28,810
I don't find they're good at it at the time
I'm filming this,

117
00:05:28,810 --> 00:05:32,080
but I do find they're good at identifying
what they're struggling with.

118
00:05:32,080 --> 00:05:33,874
It's the whole classic you should trust your

119
00:05:33,874 --> 00:05:36,320
customers with their problems but not their
solutions thing.

120
00:05:36,320 --> 00:05:40,639
I let it write the solution, not because I
want to use the solution it wrote,

121
00:05:40,639 --> 00:05:43,107
but because whatever it goes to go change is

122
00:05:43,107 --> 00:05:46,080
a really good indicator of what needs to be
adjusted.

123
00:05:46,080 --> 00:05:48,169
And you can bet your butt the first thing I

124
00:05:48,169 --> 00:05:50,355
did after this was go find that skill that
it

125
00:05:50,355 --> 00:05:52,687
changed and edit it properly in order to
push it

126
00:05:52,687 --> 00:05:55,360
across the fleet and fix this problem once
and for all.

127
00:05:55,360 --> 00:05:56,240
I do this a lot.

128
00:05:56,240 --> 00:06:00,080
Just in the last few days I was able to find
many examples.

129
00:06:00,080 --> 00:06:02,320
Here's one where I was analyzing between
threads.

130
00:06:02,560 --> 00:06:05,280
This technique works with pretty much every
harness now.

131
00:06:05,280 --> 00:06:07,049
You might not be able to get an ID easily,

132
00:06:07,049 --> 00:06:09,493
but you can just tell the agent what you
were doing in the

133
00:06:09,493 --> 00:06:12,400
other thread and it can usually look through
the history and find it.

134
00:06:12,400 --> 00:06:17,840
So I had a thread I was working on in T3
code that used the wrong tool.

135
00:06:17,840 --> 00:06:20,590
That really frustrates me when I have the
right tools

136
00:06:20,590 --> 00:06:23,600
that are provided to the model and it does
something else.

137
00:06:23,600 --> 00:06:27,440
It uses the wrong thing, it edits the wrong
file, it makes the wrong change.

138
00:06:27,440 --> 00:06:28,400
I ask why.

139
00:06:28,400 --> 00:06:30,640
In this case I said the thread used the
wrong tool.

140
00:06:30,640 --> 00:06:33,360
Figure out why and update the skills to
prevent it.

141
00:06:33,360 --> 00:06:37,647
If I should make changes to how the MCP
stuff is described as well on the T3 side,

142
00:06:37,647 --> 00:06:40,000
let me know and I'll spin up a thread for
it.

143
00:06:40,000 --> 00:06:41,920
It pointed to the skill that caused the
problem.

144
00:06:41,920 --> 00:06:45,847
Apparently my Sol loop skill, which we'll
probably talk about in the future,

145
00:06:45,847 --> 00:06:49,051
instructed the agent to use my thread send
command in T3 code,

146
00:06:49,051 --> 00:06:52,720
which caused it to create new threads when I
wanted it to do subagents.

147
00:06:52,720 --> 00:06:54,160
That was the bug I was working on here.

148
00:06:54,160 --> 00:06:55,840
I didn't even write it in the prompt because
it didn't matter.

149
00:06:55,840 --> 00:06:57,840
I trusted the thread to be able to figure it
out.

150
00:06:57,840 --> 00:07:00,418
Again, not telling the agents exactly what I
want,

151
00:07:00,418 --> 00:07:02,480
when I'm trying to figure out what's up.

152
00:07:02,480 --> 00:07:05,925
Explicit instructions are great when you
want the agent to make changes,

153
00:07:05,925 --> 00:07:08,318
but when you're exploring and trying to
figure out

154
00:07:08,318 --> 00:07:10,519
what it knows and why it behaves certain
ways,

155
00:07:10,519 --> 00:07:12,960
ask it questions and then make changes
accordingly.

156
00:07:12,960 --> 00:07:15,503
One more quick example of a more generic
type

157
00:07:15,503 --> 00:07:18,160
of problem that this way of thinking can
solve.

158
00:07:18,160 --> 00:07:21,680
I noticed that one of my threads had massive
scope creep.

159
00:07:21,680 --> 00:07:24,641
It should have been like a simple 100 or 200
line of code change,

160
00:07:24,641 --> 00:07:28,240
but I noticed the PR getting into the
thousands of lines, and I was frustrated.

161
00:07:28,240 --> 00:07:31,760
There is nothing I hate more than simple
things being unnecessarily complex.

162
00:07:31,760 --> 00:07:33,655
And agents are more than happy to write a

163
00:07:33,655 --> 00:07:35,920
ton of complex code even when they don't
need to.

164
00:07:35,920 --> 00:07:37,520
So I called it out accordingly.

165
00:07:37,520 --> 00:07:41,268
I said directly, the thread on or this
thread on my particular machine

166
00:07:41,268 --> 00:07:44,160
Cup 2 in T3 code ended up hitting massive
scope creep.

167
00:07:44,160 --> 00:07:47,508
I think it was from the Sol loops, because
again, my skill Sol Loop,

168
00:07:47,508 --> 00:07:49,920
as powerful as it is, can cause some scope
creep.

169
00:07:49,920 --> 00:07:51,121
I told it to audit it,

170
00:07:51,121 --> 00:07:55,378
figure out the causes of the scope creep and
massive inflation of the PR size,

171
00:07:55,378 --> 00:07:55,760
fix it.

172
00:07:55,760 --> 00:07:57,840
It likely needs more guidance in the skills.

173
00:07:57,840 --> 00:08:01,600
Something like, and I gave an example of
something I thought would work well.

174
00:08:01,600 --> 00:08:05,040
Don't let these review loops constantly
expand the size and scope of the changes.

175
00:08:05,040 --> 00:08:08,587
Keep an eye out for unnecessary tests,
guarding for edge cases that don't matter,

176
00:08:08,587 --> 00:08:11,040
unnecessary levels of integration or
compatibility, etc.

177
00:08:11,280 --> 00:08:13,600
Keep changes simple wherever you reasonably
can.

178
00:08:13,600 --> 00:08:15,680
I got a pretty useful response here from
Claude.

179
00:08:15,680 --> 00:08:18,720
I fixed the skills and installed them on all
11 boxes.

180
00:08:18,720 --> 00:08:21,265
You are right that the Sol loop caused part
of it,

181
00:08:21,265 --> 00:08:23,200
the other part came before any review.

182
00:08:23,200 --> 00:08:26,400
And it went through and described where
these problems came from.

183
00:08:26,400 --> 00:08:30,240
It started with this PR that had 271 lines
added and 13 removed.

184
00:08:30,240 --> 00:08:32,320
The fix it needed was only about 15 lines.

185
00:08:32,320 --> 00:08:34,965
When delegate task gets provider
unavailable,

186
00:08:34,965 --> 00:08:38,080
it should recheck the one provider and try
once more.

187
00:08:38,080 --> 00:08:40,560
Very useful description of what happened
there.

188
00:08:40,560 --> 00:08:43,001
The first version was small before Sol saw
it,

189
00:08:43,001 --> 00:08:45,920
then the agent's own review brief asked for
more scope.

190
00:08:45,920 --> 00:08:51,600
It asked Sol whether any other path should
be covered and about probe storm risks.

191
00:08:51,600 --> 00:08:55,360
The skills brief template also ask Sol to
look for quote missed cases.

192
00:08:55,360 --> 00:08:58,559
So it got 90 more lines after the first
round of review and

193
00:08:58,559 --> 00:09:01,920
then it kept going more and more until the
PR got way too big.

194
00:09:01,920 --> 00:09:07,120
What I'm trying to say here is to start from
a core of what is the problem.

195
00:09:07,120 --> 00:09:10,085
So step one should always be ask an agent to
make the change in

196
00:09:10,085 --> 00:09:13,850
the code base without giving it access to
anything special and see what it does,

197
00:09:13,850 --> 00:09:15,920
see where it struggles, see what goes wrong.

198
00:09:15,920 --> 00:09:17,746
This is similar to how I worked at Amazon,

199
00:09:17,746 --> 00:09:19,703
where I would give a ticket to a new hire
who

200
00:09:19,703 --> 00:09:22,138
didn't know where anything was yet and then
set them off

201
00:09:22,138 --> 00:09:24,182
to see where they ended up and have them ask
me

202
00:09:24,182 --> 00:09:26,400
a bunch of questions on things as they go to
do it.

203
00:09:26,400 --> 00:09:28,855
If you've somehow literally never fired off
an agent in

204
00:09:28,855 --> 00:09:31,176
your code base or you don't have any like
AGENTS.md,

205
00:09:31,176 --> 00:09:34,880
CLAUDE.md stuff like that there, this is a
great example of a first prompt to send.

206
00:09:34,880 --> 00:09:37,040
I want you to work on ticket number,
whatever.

207
00:09:37,040 --> 00:09:39,200
Explore the code base and propose a fix.

208
00:09:39,200 --> 00:09:42,560
If you struggle to find anything or you find
something confusing, let me know.

209
00:09:42,560 --> 00:09:44,800
Don't be afraid to ask dumb questions.

210
00:09:44,800 --> 00:09:46,910
This type of prompt can get you a lot of

211
00:09:46,910 --> 00:09:49,600
good insights on how your agent sees the
code base.

212
00:09:49,600 --> 00:09:51,600
And then from there, if it has questions,

213
00:09:51,600 --> 00:09:54,332
you can answer them or if it has a proposed
fix that you

214
00:09:54,332 --> 00:09:57,112
think is good you can tell it to go
implement it and then

215
00:09:57,112 --> 00:10:00,185
you can see what it does and if it does
something confusing you

216
00:10:00,185 --> 00:10:03,600
can go read through the transcript piece by
piece or you can just ask.

217
00:10:03,600 --> 00:10:07,200
And if you notice that your threads are
consistently falling into the same holes.

218
00:10:07,200 --> 00:10:09,499
Maybe they are wasting all of your CPU and
they try to

219
00:10:09,499 --> 00:10:12,480
run a dev server they shouldn't and it's
causing the machine to crash.

220
00:10:12,480 --> 00:10:15,066
Maybe they're running the typecheck command
at the wrong time

221
00:10:15,066 --> 00:10:17,440
and it's making builds take way longer than
they should.

222
00:10:17,440 --> 00:10:20,732
Maybe they're pushing a pull request but in
the wrong format and they're getting

223
00:10:20,732 --> 00:10:22,831
auto-closed because they're not following
the rules

224
00:10:22,831 --> 00:10:24,560
that you have for your templates properly.

225
00:10:24,560 --> 00:10:27,440
Whatever it is, ask why.

226
00:10:27,440 --> 00:10:29,491
Just ask the agent why it did a thing

227
00:10:29,491 --> 00:10:33,040
and you will find surprisingly useful
answers when that happens.

228
00:10:33,040 --> 00:10:37,040
And then from there, you can start
microadjusting to get it the right way.

229
00:10:37,040 --> 00:10:39,149
If it's something simple like it didn't use

230
00:10:39,149 --> 00:10:41,503
the right template when it filed a pull
request,

231
00:10:41,503 --> 00:10:45,280
you can make a small change to the global
AGENTS.md or CLAUDE.md to fix that.

232
00:10:45,280 --> 00:10:48,720
Just tell it, hey, when you make PRs, make
sure they use this template.

233
00:10:48,720 --> 00:10:49,520
Always start here.

234
00:10:49,520 --> 00:10:52,249
If you have a more complex process like I do
where I

235
00:10:52,249 --> 00:10:55,870
want it to verify itself and babysit pull
requests in a specific way,

236
00:10:55,870 --> 00:10:57,760
then you can graduate up to a skill.

237
00:10:57,760 --> 00:11:01,144
And with the skill, you can indicate when
the model should use it

238
00:11:01,144 --> 00:11:04,320
with a simple description that says use this
when X, Y, or Z.

239
00:11:04,320 --> 00:11:05,542
And then that is, again,

240
00:11:05,542 --> 00:11:09,360
bringing in certain context that the model
doesn't have to generate itself.

241
00:11:09,360 --> 00:11:11,564
I think people are a little too fixated on

242
00:11:11,564 --> 00:11:14,713
the idea of helping the model get somewhere
slightly faster,

243
00:11:14,713 --> 00:11:17,547
but generally speaking, it can read your
package JSON,

244
00:11:17,547 --> 00:11:20,906
it can read the hierarchy of files and
folders in the code base,

245
00:11:20,906 --> 00:11:24,160
it can look for the specific string that was
in the screenshot

246
00:11:24,160 --> 00:11:27,360
you gave it and figure out where in the code
base that lives.

247
00:11:27,360 --> 00:11:29,066
If you try to give it a map of the

248
00:11:29,066 --> 00:11:31,875
code base in your guidance and in your
knowledge system,

249
00:11:31,875 --> 00:11:33,731
you are giving the model an outdated,

250
00:11:33,731 --> 00:11:36,640
broken version of a thing that it can
already find itself.

251
00:11:36,640 --> 00:11:39,920
What you need to do is steer it away from
the scary parts.

252
00:11:39,920 --> 00:11:41,990
Stop thinking of your knowledge system as

253
00:11:41,990 --> 00:11:44,160
the only way someone can figure things out.

254
00:11:44,160 --> 00:11:48,320
Instead, think of it as like guardrails and
walls of a bouncy house.

255
00:11:48,320 --> 00:11:50,363
You just wanna make sure it's not going to
hurt

256
00:11:50,363 --> 00:11:52,320
itself as it gets to where you want it to
go.

257
00:11:52,320 --> 00:11:54,720
I want to jump in front of a lot of the
mistakes

258
00:11:54,720 --> 00:11:57,920
I see people making when they start going
down this rabbit hole.

259
00:11:57,920 --> 00:12:01,007
If you think the solution to the mistakes
your agents are

260
00:12:01,007 --> 00:12:04,365
making is leaving a bunch of comments all
over your code base,

261
00:12:04,365 --> 00:12:07,723
writing a manifesto of where everything
lives in the codebase,

262
00:12:07,723 --> 00:12:09,727
or trying your hardest to add so many

263
00:12:09,727 --> 00:12:12,327
unit tests the agent could never make a
mistake,

264
00:12:12,327 --> 00:12:15,360
you're very far from the way things are
going right now.

265
00:12:15,360 --> 00:12:18,663
The best solution is almost always to make
the code base

266
00:12:18,663 --> 00:12:22,320
simpler and easier for both you and your
agents to understand.

267
00:12:22,320 --> 00:12:25,450
The vast majority of the problems I have run
into with agents

268
00:12:25,450 --> 00:12:29,503
making mistakes aren't because I didn't have
the right knowledge in the system,

269
00:12:29,503 --> 00:12:31,760
it's because the thing itself was confusing.

270
00:12:31,760 --> 00:12:35,304
Even if I did write up how it worked,
somewhere where the agent could access,

271
00:12:35,304 --> 00:12:38,480
it would still be confusing, it would still
be unnecessarily complex.

272
00:12:38,480 --> 00:12:40,285
And the vast majority of the time,

273
00:12:40,285 --> 00:12:44,638
whether this is at a big Fortune 500 company
or a small startup or a side project,

274
00:12:44,638 --> 00:12:47,292
you can almost always simplify things so
that both

275
00:12:47,292 --> 00:12:50,000
the humans and the agents can understand it
better.

276
00:12:50,000 --> 00:12:52,440
*** from Cursor and now SpaceX AI has had

277
00:12:52,440 --> 00:12:55,600
some really good commentary on what's going
on right now.

278
00:12:55,600 --> 00:12:58,400
This is a post she made about how to trust
your agent's work more.

279
00:12:58,400 --> 00:13:01,920
She also says you can just copy the post or
link and give it directly to your bot.

280
00:13:01,920 --> 00:13:02,880
Highly recommend doing that.

281
00:13:02,880 --> 00:13:04,560
You'll be surprised what it can find.

282
00:13:04,560 --> 00:13:06,080
In fact, I will do that now.

283
00:13:06,080 --> 00:13:08,281
I'm literally just grabbing the tweet,
pasting the link,

284
00:13:08,281 --> 00:13:10,639
screenshots because I know that GPT models
aren't as good at

285
00:13:10,639 --> 00:13:13,272
accessing X because uh somebody wants to
keep them from doing that,

286
00:13:13,272 --> 00:13:15,158
and just telling it use the principles from
this

287
00:13:15,158 --> 00:13:17,280
tweet to audit the code base and suggest
improvements.

288
00:13:17,280 --> 00:13:18,640
And while that runs, we can read it.

289
00:13:18,640 --> 00:13:20,883
The first step to making sure you can trust
your

290
00:13:20,883 --> 00:13:23,360
agent's work more is to improve the code
base itself.

291
00:13:23,360 --> 00:13:26,204
This is just good software engineering,
better architecture,

292
00:13:26,204 --> 00:13:28,480
stronger types and invariants, clear
boundaries.

293
00:13:28,480 --> 00:13:31,707
Agents love to copy what exists and take the
shortest path,

294
00:13:31,707 --> 00:13:33,840
so make the easy thing the right thing.

295
00:13:33,840 --> 00:13:36,240
This is one of the most important pieces.

296
00:13:36,240 --> 00:13:40,901
I find everyone is trying to cover up the
absolute chitchos that are their code

297
00:13:40,901 --> 00:13:43,910
base by having these complex knowledge
systems that

298
00:13:43,910 --> 00:13:46,860
re-encode all of the complex spaghetti they
wrote,

299
00:13:46,860 --> 00:13:49,280
there is no excuse for spaghetti anymore.

300
00:13:49,280 --> 00:13:52,468
Agents can go through that spaghetti and
trim it down to

301
00:13:52,468 --> 00:13:55,200
be way simpler if you guide them there to do
it.

302
00:13:55,200 --> 00:13:58,461
So if you have your agents doing stupid
things or pushing

303
00:13:58,461 --> 00:14:02,466
spaghetti all across your code base or
making obviously bad decisions,

304
00:14:02,466 --> 00:14:04,240
you need to fix your code base.

305
00:14:04,240 --> 00:14:07,032
Categorically eliminate the problems through
the code and

306
00:14:07,032 --> 00:14:09,040
you won't need to think about this again.

307
00:14:09,040 --> 00:14:11,187
After you have exhausted all of the things
you

308
00:14:11,187 --> 00:14:13,240
can do to make the code base itself simpler,

309
00:14:13,240 --> 00:14:15,107
then you can go into these other layers,

310
00:14:15,107 --> 00:14:18,000
but you should think of each one of these as
a bigger failure.

311
00:14:18,000 --> 00:14:20,320
The more you can do at the top level, the
better.

312
00:14:20,320 --> 00:14:24,396
If you somehow fail to make things behave
better there, then go to the next layer,

313
00:14:24,396 --> 00:14:25,440
like static analysis.

314
00:14:25,440 --> 00:14:28,880
This is where things like lint rules, tests,
CI and verification all come in.

315
00:14:28,880 --> 00:14:32,240
It's a token efficient way to catch these
types of issues ahead of time.

316
00:14:32,240 --> 00:14:35,439
If you have CI that will test that the app
behaves when a user

317
00:14:35,439 --> 00:14:39,619
clicks a certain place that it hits the
database and shows them the right result,

318
00:14:39,619 --> 00:14:42,406
that can be verified without an agent having
to do it,

319
00:14:42,406 --> 00:14:45,760
which can be cheap and also help the agent
make better decisions.

320
00:14:45,760 --> 00:14:46,482
Generally speaking,

321
00:14:46,482 --> 00:14:49,178
you should just make it so it writes the
code correctly the first time,

322
00:14:49,178 --> 00:14:51,760
but these tools help prevent it from making
those types of mistakes.

323
00:14:51,760 --> 00:14:53,909
If those two things are not enough,

324
00:14:53,909 --> 00:14:57,040
then you must begrudgingly go into the prose
layer.

325
00:14:57,040 --> 00:14:59,686
This is where a lot of the knowledge system
stuff lives,

326
00:14:59,686 --> 00:15:01,245
and that's why I'm so against it,

327
00:15:01,245 --> 00:15:04,080
because the knowledge system is two layers
of failures deep.

328
00:15:04,080 --> 00:15:06,804
Putting something about your code base in
your knowledge

329
00:15:06,804 --> 00:15:09,479
system means that you're admitting that your
code is so

330
00:15:09,479 --> 00:15:11,327
bad and that your linting and CI is so

331
00:15:11,327 --> 00:15:13,760
bad that they can't steer the agent the
right way.

332
00:15:13,760 --> 00:15:17,533
So now you have to do it through unnecessary
comments, rules,

333
00:15:17,533 --> 00:15:21,120
AGENTS.md file changes, skills, all those
types of things.

334
00:15:21,120 --> 00:15:22,480
They can't be enforced.

335
00:15:22,480 --> 00:15:27,120
They require the agent itself to do its own
judgment around these things.

336
00:15:27,120 --> 00:15:29,200
Use them minimally, but if you do need to

337
00:15:30,160 --> 00:15:32,697
you can and then the final layer the one
that slows

338
00:15:32,697 --> 00:15:35,333
you down the most human review at the rate
agents are

339
00:15:35,333 --> 00:15:38,815
landing code it's pretty much impossible to
thoroughly review every PR

340
00:15:38,815 --> 00:15:42,098
so spend the effort you would have on the
highest leverage points,

341
00:15:42,098 --> 00:15:45,680
design environments where the agents will do
the right thing by default.

342
00:15:45,680 --> 00:15:49,051
I was just at the bottom here of looking
through your Slack and chat messages

343
00:15:49,051 --> 00:15:50,758
and code review comments to find places

344
00:15:50,758 --> 00:15:53,210
where you're giving PR feedback code
architecture stuff,

345
00:15:53,210 --> 00:15:56,712
recalling all the past things, and then
going through the code base and auditing

346
00:15:56,712 --> 00:15:59,120
to clean it up to prevent these mistakes
going forward.

347
00:15:59,120 --> 00:15:59,920
Great advice.

348
00:15:59,920 --> 00:16:00,720
Highly recommend it.

349
00:16:00,720 --> 00:16:04,320
It already called out useful things for me
to go adjust in my own code base.

350
00:16:04,320 --> 00:16:06,139
Apparently we already have good rules,

351
00:16:06,139 --> 00:16:10,160
but we need to make them more automatic so
they apply more often and more correctly.

352
00:16:10,160 --> 00:16:13,135
We need to increase visibility on missing
provider coverage,

353
00:16:13,135 --> 00:16:17,300
give agents a single command for doing
things like selecting the right local
checks,

354
00:16:17,300 --> 00:16:20,720
convert mechanical reviews into Lint, and
then reduce repeated prose.

355
00:16:20,800 --> 00:16:23,047
We have a whole set of guidelines for how

356
00:16:23,047 --> 00:16:26,720
to review things that change our effect
service layer, for example.

357
00:16:26,720 --> 00:16:30,638
That's all markdown-based guidance that this
believes we can actually lint for,

358
00:16:30,638 --> 00:16:31,680
which is cool to see.

359
00:16:31,680 --> 00:16:33,760
Definitely should go do those things
afterwards.

360
00:16:33,760 --> 00:16:36,400
I have one last prompt to show you before we
wrap up.

361
00:16:36,400 --> 00:16:39,041
What are the most common issues my agents
